1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,066
In this final video, using SDK 0.1.0,
we'll integrate Soroban Identity into an

2
00:00:09,066 --> 00:00:09,763
app.

3
00:00:09,763 --> 00:00:20,921
The example is a trading feature that only
verified users can use. Alice has a KYC

4
00:00:20,921 --> 00:00:25,105
credential and gets in; Bob doesn't.

5
00:00:25,105 --> 00:00:35,566
We'll build the front end check, the back
end enforcement, and a sybil check, then

6
00:00:35,566 --> 00:00:39,750
talk about errors, performance and
mainnet.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,693
Here's the shape of it.

8
00:00:44,693 --> 00:01:00,651
The browser connects a wallet and does a
check so the UI can show the right screen.

9
00:01:00,651 --> 00:01:13,792
But the browser can't be trusted, so our
API does the authoritative check before

10
00:01:13,792 --> 00:01:17,547
doing anything that matters.

11
00:01:17,547 --> 00:01:29,750
Both talk to the same contracts through
Soroban RPC, using the same SDK.

12
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:42,821
Install the SDK alongside the Stellar SDK.

13
00:01:42,821 --> 00:02:08,464
Then create one config object for the
whole app. Start from the testnet or

14
00:02:08,464 --> 00:02:30,444
mainnet preset and fill in the three
contract IDs from environment variables.

15
00:02:30,444 --> 00:02:57,918
Keeping IDs in env vars means switching
networks is a config change, not a code

16
00:02:57,918 --> 00:02:59,750
change.

17
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:13,165
For wallets, the reference frontend has a
useWallet hook built on Stellar Wallets

18
00:03:13,165 --> 00:03:27,343
Kit, so it supports Freighter and other
Stellar wallets. Feel free to copy it.

19
00:03:27,343 --> 00:03:44,559
All we need from the wallet right now is
the user's public key. Every check in this

20
00:03:44,559 --> 00:03:59,750
video is a read, so the user never has to
sign anything to be verified.

21
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:19,958
Here's a hook that answers one question:
can this user trade?

22
00:04:19,958 --> 00:04:48,989
First, does the address have an active
DID? If not, we send them to create one.

23
00:04:48,989 --> 00:05:14,390
Then we list their credentials and look
for a KYC credential that isn't revoked,

24
00:05:14,390 --> 00:05:25,277
has become active, and hasn't expired.

25
00:05:25,277 --> 00:05:54,307
We return a small state machine, so the UI
can show loading, create your DID, get

26
00:05:54,307 --> 00:05:59,750
verified, or unlocked.

27
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:12,849
In the component, switch on the gate.

28
00:06:12,849 --> 00:06:36,711
Connect as Alice, and the trade form
appears. Switch Freighter to Bob, and

29
00:06:36,711 --> 00:06:49,559
we're asked to create a DID first.

30
00:06:49,559 --> 00:07:09,750
Each state gets a next step, never just a
locked door.

31
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:24,660
Any registered issuer can issue a KYC
credential, but your compliance team

32
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:31,991
probably trusts only some of them.

33
00:07:31,991 --> 00:07:45,429
Keep your own allowlist of issuer
addresses and check the credential's

34
00:07:45,429 --> 00:07:49,094
issuer against it.

35
00:07:49,094 --> 00:08:06,198
This also protects you if an issuer is
later removed from the registry, because

36
00:08:06,198 --> 00:08:14,750
credentials it already issued stay valid
on-chain.

37
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:34,506
Now watch this. With devtools I can force
the hook to return OK, and Bob sees the

38
00:08:34,506 --> 00:08:36,801
trade form.

39
00:08:36,801 --> 00:08:50,571
Front-end checks are for user experience.
Anything that matters, like accepting an

40
00:08:50,571 --> 00:08:59,750
order, must be checked again on the
server.

41
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:21,448
On the server, write a middleware that
runs before any protected route.

42
00:09:21,448 --> 00:09:46,470
Two checks, in this order. First, fetch
the credential and make sure it actually

43
00:09:46,470 --> 00:10:13,280
belongs to the logged-in address, is a KYC
credential, and comes from an issuer we

44
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:29,366
accept. Otherwise anyone could present
someone else's credential ID.

45
00:10:29,366 --> 00:10:45,451
Second, verify it, so we catch revocation
and expiry.

46
00:10:45,451 --> 00:10:59,750
If either fails, return 403 with the
reason.

47
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:08,156
That middleware assumes req.auth.address
really belongs to the caller. Soroban

48
00:11:08,156 --> 00:11:13,049
Identity doesn't handle login for you.

49
00:11:13,049 --> 00:11:24,467
The usual pattern is a challenge: the
server sends a random nonce, the wallet

50
00:11:24,467 --> 00:11:34,254
signs it, and the server checks the
signature against the address before

51
00:11:34,254 --> 00:11:36,701
starting a session.

52
00:11:36,701 --> 00:11:48,934
Only then do you trust the address, and
only then do the identity checks mean

53
00:11:48,934 --> 00:11:49,750
anything.

54
00:11:50,000 --> 00:12:04,946
For things like airdrops or voting, KYC
may be overkill but you still want one

55
00:12:04,946 --> 00:12:07,936
person, one account.

56
00:12:07,936 --> 00:12:19,893
The reputation contract's passes sybil
check does exactly that: does this address

57
00:12:19,893 --> 00:12:33,843
have at least a minimum score, from at
least a minimum number of independent

58
00:12:33,843 --> 00:12:34,839
reporters?

59
00:12:34,839 --> 00:12:48,789
Here we require a score of fifty from at
least two reporters. Needing multiple

60
00:12:48,789 --> 00:12:59,750
reporters makes it much harder to game
than a single score.

61
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:14,561
Your app can contribute reputation too.
Once the reputation admin registers your

62
00:13:14,561 --> 00:13:31,549
backend as a reporter, submit score deltas
as users do good things, like completing

63
00:13:31,549 --> 00:13:35,189
trades without disputes.

64
00:13:35,189 --> 00:13:47,323
Keep the reason short and
machine-readable. It's stored in the

65
00:13:47,323 --> 00:13:49,750
subject's history.

66
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:59,336
Everything here goes over the network, so
plan for failure.

67
00:13:59,336 --> 00:14:13,340
RPC calls can time out or be rate-limited.
Catch errors and show a retry state

68
00:14:13,340 --> 00:14:24,543
instead of treating failure as not
verified. You don't want a flaky

69
00:14:24,543 --> 00:14:32,945
connection to tell a verified user they
aren't verified.

70
00:14:32,945 --> 00:14:46,949
On the server it's the other way round:
fail closed. If you can't verify, don't

71
00:14:46,949 --> 00:14:49,750
allow the action.

72
00:14:50,000 --> 00:15:03,528
Each check is a simulation round trip,
typically a few hundred milliseconds.

73
00:15:03,528 --> 00:15:22,693
On the server, cache results per address
for a short time, a minute or so, so a

74
00:15:22,693 --> 00:15:33,967
burst of requests doesn't mean a burst of
RPC calls.

75
00:15:33,967 --> 00:15:45,241
But always re-verify right before
high-value actions, since a revocation

76
00:15:45,241 --> 00:15:49,750
should take effect immediately.

77
00:15:50,000 --> 00:16:00,731
If you're the one doing KYC, you're also
an issuer.

78
00:16:00,731 --> 00:16:16,827
A common setup: your KYC vendor calls a
webhook when a user passes, and your

79
00:16:16,827 --> 00:16:31,850
backend hashes the claims and issues the
credential with a key registered as an

80
00:16:31,850 --> 00:16:32,923
issuer.

81
00:16:32,923 --> 00:16:50,092
Keep the issuer's secret key in a secrets
manager, never in the front end, and store

82
00:16:50,092 --> 00:16:59,750
the credential ID so you can revoke it
later.

83
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:09,089
When you're ready for mainnet, here's the
checklist.

84
00:17:09,089 --> 00:17:23,858
Deploy the contracts to mainnet and
initialize them with an admin you control,

85
00:17:23,858 --> 00:17:28,402
ideally a multisig account.

86
00:17:28,402 --> 00:17:35,218
Register your production issuers and
reporters.

87
00:17:35,218 --> 00:17:51,123
Switch your config to MAINNET CONFIG with
the new contract IDs, and update your

88
00:17:51,123 --> 00:18:00,212
accepted issuers list, because mainnet
addresses are different.

89
00:18:00,212 --> 00:18:16,117
Make sure wallets are on the right
network, since a user on testnet will

90
00:18:16,117 --> 00:18:19,525
simply look unverified.

91
00:18:19,525 --> 00:18:29,750
And use a reliable RPC provider for
production traffic.

92
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:44,132
For automated tests, mock the SDK clients
rather than hitting the network.

93
00:18:44,132 --> 00:18:59,441
Cover each state: no DID, no credential,
each failure reason including not yet

94
00:18:59,441 --> 00:19:14,750
active, and valid. Then run a manual pass
on testnet before every release.

95
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:27,647
That's the whole series. You've seen the
protocol, deployed it, managed DIDs and

96
00:19:27,647 --> 00:19:38,348
credentials, and integrated it into an app
with proper server-side enforcement.

97
00:19:38,348 --> 00:19:53,913
Links to the code, the docs and the rest
of the playlist are in the description.

98
00:19:53,913 --> 00:19:59,750
Thanks for watching, and happy building.
